// Every message satl can say about a program, as data. DESIGN §9 is the
// specification and this file is the half a compiler can check.
//
// THERE IS NO GENERATOR AND NO BUILD STEP. This is an X-macro list, the same
// mechanism words.def uses and FORMAT/CXX.md §7 describes: a consumer defines
// SAT_CODE, includes this file, and undefines it again. The enum, the sentence
// table, the severity table, the arity table, the invariants and
// `satl --errors` therefore all come from ONE list and cannot drift apart.
//
// WHAT THE FIRST SATELLITE DID INSTEAD IS THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS. DESIGN
// §9 counts it: 199 `fail()` call sites composing 224 distinct string literals
// between them, with no codes, no stack and no suggestions. A message that
// lives at its call site cannot be looked up, cannot be counted, and cannot be
// found again when somebody wants to know whether two sites say the same thing
// in two ways. Every row below is one sentence in one place.

// --- how a row is read ------------------------------------------------------
//
//     SAT_CODE(number, ident, severity, text)
//
// THE NUMBER IS A COLUMN HERE AND IT IS A POSITION IN words.def, and the
// difference is deliberate rather than an inconsistency between two registries.
// A word's number is what a program MEANS -- DESIGN §4.3 freezes registration
// order because inserting a row silently renumbers every sibling and every
// already-written program with it. An error code is what a PERSON LOOKS UP: it
// has to survive a row being deleted, has to leave gaps where a milestone
// reserved a block, and has to be assignable out of order. Those are opposite
// requirements and they get opposite encodings. What replaces "a number is a
// position" as the safety net is words_invariants.hpp's trick pointed at this
// table instead: the numbers must ASCEND, which catches an insertion, and the
// switch in codes.hpp catches a duplicate as `error: duplicate case value`.
//
// `severity` is one of two things:
//
//     SAT_ERROR   something is wrong and satl did not do what was asked
//     SAT_NOTE    a second place worth looking at, or a fact about the first
//
// A NOTE IS A ROW HERE AND NOT A STRING AT A CALL SITE, which is the whole
// argument of this file applied to the half that is easiest to exempt from it.
// DESIGN §9 asks for "notes carrying their own spans"; a note whose sentence
// lived beside the error that raises it would be the 224 literals again, at
// half the volume and with none of the visibility.
//
// `text` is the sentence, with `{1}`, `{2}`, `{3}` where the site fills a hole.
// THE ARITY IS NOT A COLUMN -- it is the highest hole in the text, counted at
// compile time, and codes.hpp asserts the holes run 1..N with none missing. A
// column would be a second place the same fact lives, and the one that goes
// stale is always the column.
//
// AND THE ARITY IS CHECKED AT THE CALL SITE, which is the property this whole
// arrangement is for: errors::make<Code::X>(span, a, b) is a template, so a
// site that passes two arguments to a three-hole sentence is a compile error
// naming the code. The first satellite could not have had that check, because
// the sentence was the argument.

// --- the blocks -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The first two digits say WHO IS COMPLAINING, which is what makes a code worth
// four digits rather than two. A reader who has S0231 in front of them knows it
// came from the parser before they look it up, and a milestone that adds codes
// takes a block instead of taking the next free number and interleaving itself
// with everybody else.
//
//     S01xx   the lexer                     DESIGN §5      M3
//     S02xx   the parser                    DESIGN §6      M4
//     S03xx   the `.satc` cache             SATC.md        M4.5
//     S04xx   the file satl was given                      M5
//     S05xx   resolve -- names and slots    DESIGN §7      M7       reserved
//     S06xx   numbers                       DESIGN §8.1    M8       reserved
//     S07xx   the evaluator                                M9/M10   reserved
//     S08xx   the machine limits            PLAN §4.5      M6
//
// The reserved blocks are written down and NOT populated. A block reserved in a
// comment costs nothing and stops the thing this file exists to stop, which is
// two milestones reaching for the same number on the same day.
//
// M6 TOOK S08xx AND NOT S04xx, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE ASKED FOR THAT
// CHECK BY NAME. It said "S08xx being the free one is the answer only until
// somebody checks whether the machine limits belong beside S04xx -- the file
// satl was given -- instead." Checked, 2026-08-30, and they do not. S04xx's own
// block note says what it is: "the command line was well formed and named a
// file, and the file is where it stopped" -- a file the USER GAVE satl, holding
// a program the user wrote. A satellite_config.ini is a file satl WENT LOOKING
// FOR, beside its own binary, holding settings an installer wrote; the ordinary
// run never names it and never sees it. The first two digits say who is
// complaining, and the complainer here is the limits reader rather than the arm
// that opens what it was handed.
//
// IT IS OUT OF ORDER WITH THE RESERVATIONS ABOVE, DELIBERATELY. S05, S06 and S07
// are M7's, M8's and M9's, and M6 lands before all three -- so the block numbers
// no longer run in the order the milestones do. That is what a reserved block IS
// for: this file's whole argument is that a block is taken and written down so
// that "two milestones reaching for the same number on the same day" cannot
// happen, and honouring three reservations by taking the fourth number is that
// working, not a defect in it. A code is a column and not a position (see the
// paragraph on how a row is read), so nothing about the numbering says the
// blocks are in build order, and nothing should be inferred from it.

#ifndef SAT_CODE
#define SAT_CODE(number, ident, severity, text)
#endif

// The two severities, as values, so the numbers live here and only there --
// read back as errors::kError and errors::kNote. NOT #undef'd at the bottom
// with the macro above, for that reason.
#ifndef SAT_ERROR
#define SAT_ERROR 1
#endif
#ifndef SAT_NOTE
#define SAT_NOTE 0
#endif

// --- S01xx -- the lexer -----------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE ERROR, AND THAT IS THE LEXER'S DESIGN RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. DESIGN
// §5.6 says a byte the code table has no entry for becomes a Punct holding that
// byte rather than an error, "because the lexer's job is to describe the file,
// and deciding that a character is meaningless is the parser's". So the only
// thing that can stop a lex is a string literal with no end.

SAT_CODE(101, LEX_UNTERMINATED_STRING, SAT_ERROR,
         "this `\"` opens a string literal and nothing closes it before the "
         "end of the line -- put a `\"` where the text is meant to stop")

SAT_CODE(102, LEX_LINE_ENDS_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "the line ends here, and a string literal cannot carry on to the "
         "next one")

// --- S02xx -- the parser ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 200-209 is the cursor and the four loops, 210-219 statements, 220-229 types,
// 230-239 expressions, 240-249 names. The gaps are on purpose; see the block
// note above.

SAT_CODE(201, PARSE_EXPECTED_PUNCT, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected `{1}` {2}, and found {3} instead")

SAT_CODE(202, PARSE_EXPECTED_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected {1}, and found {2} instead")

SAT_CODE(203, PARSE_STATEMENT_ALREADY_ENDED, SAT_ERROR,
         "a statement ends at the end of its line, and {1} is a second one on "
         "the same line -- put it on a line of its own")

SAT_CODE(204, PARSE_EXPECTED_DECLARATION, SAT_ERROR,
         "a file holds satellite.include, satellite.capsule, "
         "satellite.spacesuit and satellite.library.<name>, and {1} is none of "
         "them")

SAT_CODE(205, PARSE_EXPECTED_STATEMENT, SAT_ERROR,
         "a block holds statements, and {1} does not begin one")

SAT_CODE(206, PARSE_EXPECTED_SUIT_ITEM, SAT_ERROR,
         "a spacesuit holds fields, capsules, and satellite.protected or "
         "satellite.public sections, and {1} is none of them")

SAT_CODE(207, PARSE_EXPECTED_FIELD_OR_CAPSULE, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected a field or a satellite.capsule, and found {1}")

// SAID RATHER THAN SHOWN BY THE OUTPUT SIMPLY STOPPING. parser.cpp reports at
// most 20 errors and then leaves the rest of the file unread; without this the
// twenty-first problem is invisible and the file looks understood.
SAT_CODE(208, PARSE_TOO_MANY_ERRORS, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} problems were reported and the rest of the file was not read -- "
         "fix these and run it again")

SAT_CODE(210, PARSE_STATEMENT_NEEDS_A_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.statement is written with one of if, else, while or for "
         "after it")

// NAMED RATHER THAN LUMPED IN WITH "no such statement", because a stray `else`
// is a real thing a person writes and the useful sentence is about the `if`
// and not about the word.
SAT_CODE(211, PARSE_ELSE_WITHOUT_IF, SAT_ERROR,
         "this satellite.statement.else has no satellite.statement.if in front "
         "of it to belong to")

SAT_CODE(212, PARSE_NO_SUCH_STATEMENT, SAT_ERROR,
         "no statement is spelled `{1}` -- satellite.statement has if, else, "
         "while and for")

SAT_CODE(213, PARSE_DECLARATION_IN_BLOCK, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is a declaration and goes at the top of a file, not "
         "inside a block")

SAT_CODE(214, PARSE_ELSE_NEEDS_A_BLOCK, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.statement.else is followed by a block or by another "
         "satellite.statement.if, and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(215, PARSE_ASSIGN_TARGET, SAT_ERROR,
         "the left of an assignment has to name somewhere to write, and {1} "
         "does not")

SAT_CODE(220, PARSE_NOT_A_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is not a type -- a type path is "
         "satellite.variable.<name> or satellite.container.<name>")

SAT_CODE(221, PARSE_EXPECTED_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "a type is satellite.variable.<name>, satellite.container.<name>, "
         "satellite itself, or the name of a spacesuit -- and {1} is none of "
         "them")

// `>=` IS ONE TOKEN AND DESIGN §5.5 IS WHY IT STAYS ONE. The lexer matches it
// greedily and lexer.hpp's split_punct is deliberately never called, so the fix
// belongs in the program: a space.
SAT_CODE(222, PARSE_GENERIC_CLOSE_GE, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected `>` to close the type's arguments and found `>=`, which is "
         "one token -- write a space between the two characters")

SAT_CODE(230, PARSE_EXPECTED_SUBSCRIPT, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected an index or a slice inside the brackets, and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(231, PARSE_EXPECTED_EXPRESSION, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected an expression -- a number, a string, a name, a path, or one "
         "in brackets -- and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(240, PARSE_NOT_A_LANGUAGE_CAPSULE, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is not a capsule the language owns -- the one reserved "
         "capsule name is satellite.main")

SAT_CODE(241, PARSE_NAME_IS_LANGUAGE_OWNED, SAT_ERROR,
         "the language already owns the name {1} under {2}, so it cannot be a "
         "{3}'s name")

SAT_CODE(242, PARSE_NAME_ALREADY_DEFINED, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is already the name of something in this program, and a name is "
         "declared once")

SAT_CODE(243, PARSE_NAME_UNNUMBERABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} could not be given a number, which is satl's own limit and not "
         "anything wrong with this program")

// The parser's notes. 290-299, at the end of the block rather than beside the
// errors that raise them, because a note is reused: 290 is attached by nine
// call sites and belongs to none of them.
SAT_CODE(290, NOTE_OPENED_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "this `{1}` is the one that is not closed")

SAT_CODE(291, NOTE_FIRST_DECLARED_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} was declared here")

// --- S03xx -- the `.satc` cache ---------------------------------------------
//
// SATC.md §4's four ways a cache file can be wrong, plus the sentence all four
// end with. M4.5 wrote these as string literals and said in its own header that
// "codes are M5's to design with all of them in view"; this is that.
//
// NONE OF THEM CARRIES A SPAN, and the reason is worth stating because the
// machinery is right here and unused. A `.satc` is not a file the user wrote --
// read.cpp's own argument -- so what they can act on is the file as a whole,
// which is exactly what the location line already names. A caret pointing at
// byte 412 of a generated file tells somebody to read a file that the next
// sentence tells them to delete.

SAT_CODE(301, SATC_NOT_A_SATC, SAT_ERROR,
         "this does not begin with a `satc` line, so it is not a `.satc` at all")

SAT_CODE(302, SATC_WRONG_VERSION, SAT_ERROR,
         "this is version {1} of the `.satc` format and this satl reads "
         "version {2}, so it was written by a different satl")

SAT_CODE(303, SATC_NO_BLANK_LINE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this has no blank line after its three header lines, so where the "
         "header ends cannot be told")

SAT_CODE(304, SATC_DID_NOT_PARSE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this did not parse: {1}")

SAT_CODE(305, SATC_MARK_WITHOUT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "this has a `{1}` with no number after it, which is not something the "
         "writer can produce")

SAT_CODE(306, SATC_UNKNOWN_PATH, SAT_ERROR,
         "this names `{1}`, which this satl's numbering does not have")

SAT_CODE(390, NOTE_SATC_IGNORED, SAT_NOTE,
         "it was ignored and the source was read instead, so nothing is wrong "
         "with your program -- deleting the file is safe, because a `.satc` is "
         "only a cache")

// --- S04xx -- the file satl was given ---------------------------------------
//
// NOT THE COMMAND LINE, which is a different interface and keeps its own words
// in programs/opening.cpp. A code and a span are for something INSIDE a file;
// `satl --frobnicate` has no file and no place in one, and giving it a code
// would be a lookup number for a sentence that already names its own fix.
// This block is the seam between the two: the command line was well formed and
// named a file, and the file is where it stopped.

SAT_CODE(401, FILE_UNREADABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file could not be read -- check the path, and that this user is "
         "allowed to open it")

// --- S08xx -- the machine limits --------------------------------------------
//
// PLAN §4.5's satellite_config.ini, refused rather than ignored. M6's done-when
// asks for "a malformed file refused with a code, a span and a caret", and the
// whole file is refused on the first problem it holds -- unlike a bad `.satc`,
// which S0390 says is ignored while the source is read instead. The difference
// is what the two files ARE: a `.satc` is a cache and deleting it costs nothing,
// while a config is the only statement of how much of this machine satl may
// take, and guessing at that would be DESIGN §1.1's "behind their back" with the
// machine's memory as the stake.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THESE CARRIES A REAL SPAN, which is the difference from S03xx one
// block up. A `.satc` is generated and its span would point a person at a file
// the next sentence tells them to delete; a satellite_config.ini is TYPED, by an
// installer or by hand, and the caret lands under the character that is wrong.
//
// THE WATCHDOG HAS NO CODE HERE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. When MEMORY_MAX is
// crossed satl prints one line of plain words on stderr and goes, which is what
// M6's done-when asks for. A code is for looking up and a span is for something
// inside a file; a process that has run out of the memory it was allowed has
// neither, and rendering `satl: error S08xx:` over it would dress a shutdown up
// as a diagnostic about a file. programs/opening.cpp already keeps the words for
// everything that is about the RUN rather than about a file, and this is that.

SAT_CODE(801, CONFIG_NOT_A_SETTING, SAT_ERROR,
         "a line in this file is `NAME=VALUE`, a `#` comment or blank -- there "
         "are no [sections] -- and {1} is none of them")

SAT_CODE(802, CONFIG_NO_SUCH_SETTING, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file has no setting called `{1}` -- `satl --limits` lists every "
         "one it has")

SAT_CODE(803, CONFIG_SET_TWICE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is set twice in this file, and a setting is set once")

SAT_CODE(804, CONFIG_NOT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number and `{2}` is not one")

SAT_CODE(805, CONFIG_NEEDS_A_UNIT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is an amount of memory and `{2}` says how much without saying of "
         "what -- write `{2}GiB` for powers of 1024 or `{2}GB` for powers of "
         "1000, because 61.9 GiB and 64.9 GB are the same memory and a bare "
         "number cannot say which was meant")

SAT_CODE(806, CONFIG_NO_SUCH_UNIT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a unit -- B, KiB, MiB, GiB and TiB are powers of 1024, "
         "and KB, MB, GB and TB are powers of 1000")

SAT_CODE(807, CONFIG_TOO_SMALL, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is {2} and the smallest it may be is {3}")

SAT_CODE(808, CONFIG_TOO_LARGE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is {2} and the largest it may be is {3}")

// SEPARATE FROM S0804 BECAUSE THE FIX IS DIFFERENT AND A SENTENCE HAS TO NAME
// THE FIX. `CORE_COUNT=twelve` is answered by writing a number; `MEMORY_MAX=
// 61.9GiB` is a number, and what is wrong with it is that a ceiling rounded to
// a whole byte behind the user's back is one nobody can predict -- so the fix
// is the next unit down, where the amount they mean is exact. Answering both
// with "is a whole number" would have been one row saying two things, which is
// the failure this file exists to make visible rather than one it may commit.
SAT_CODE(809, CONFIG_NOT_A_WHOLE_AMOUNT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number of units and `{2}` is not one -- write the "
         "next unit down, so that the byte count is exact instead of rounded")

// THE THIRD THING A MACHINE SETTING'S VALUE MAY BE WRONG ABOUT, and it is a
// different sentence from S0804 for the reason S0809 is: the sentence has to
// name the fix, and here there are two fixes because there are two things the
// value is allowed to be. `CORE_COUNT=twelve` could have meant a number or it
// could have meant the machine, and a message saying only "is a whole number"
// would send somebody to look up a core count they did not want to write down.
//
// {2} IS THE PATH THIS SETTING TAKES AND IT IS NOT A FIXED STRING. Each of the
// three has exactly one -- DESIGN §7.7's pairing -- so the row is the same
// sentence with a different answer in it, and config_internal.hpp's kSettings
// is where the answer comes from. A file that names the WRONG one of the three
// lands here too, which is right: `CORE_COUNT=arguments.memory.total` is not a
// core count, and the message names the one that is.
//
// This row cannot be raised from example/broken_config.ini -- all three
// settings are spent there on S0805, S0807 and S0808, and naming one twice
// raises S0803 instead -- so tests/limits_test raises it from a string, which
// is that file's own established answer for S0806, S0809 and S0891.
SAT_CODE(810, CONFIG_NOT_A_FACT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number or `{2}`, which is what this machine says -- "
         "and `{3}` is neither")

SAT_CODE(890, NOTE_CONFIG_SET_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} was set here first")

// The parser's S0208 one registry on, and the same argument: a reader stops
// after twenty problems, and without this the twenty-first is invisible and the
// rest of the file looks understood. A config file is a dozen lines and will
// almost never reach it -- which is exactly when a cap goes unnoticed, so it is
// said out loud rather than left to a reader who pointed satl at the wrong file.
SAT_CODE(891, NOTE_CONFIG_TOO_MANY, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} problems were reported and the rest of this file was not read -- "
         "fix these and run it again")

#undef SAT_CODE
